#!/bin/sh
#
# script/preflight-queue: replay the merge queue on this machine, in order, before a group build does.
#
#     script/preflight-queue              # --dry-run: test every entry, report, change nothing
#     script/preflight-queue --act        # the same walk, then comment on and dequeue what is red
#     script/preflight-queue --no-hvf     # skip the HVF leg on main's tip (it runs first by default)
#
# # Why this exists
#
# Per-pull-request CI tests a branch against the `main` it was cut from, or at best against the
# `main` of its last push. The merge queue tests it against `main` plus every entry ahead of it, in
# groups of up to five, and a group with one red member fails as a whole and is rebuilt without it.
# So a pull request can be green on its own page and still red in the queue, and nothing before the
# group build can see that, because nothing before it builds the stack.
#
# **The evidence, from 2026-09-24.** A lane replayed the queue by hand: 23 entries in about 40
# minutes, each merged onto the green ones ahead of it. It found three pull requests (#1194, #1222,
# #1182) that pass CI alone and fail on top of the entries ahead of them. Each of those would have
# failed a merge group of five, and a failed group costs roughly an hour of starved runner time per
# member, since every member's build is thrown away and redone. Forty minutes of one laptop against
# up to fifteen runner-hours is the trade this script makes repeatable, rather than something one
# lane happened to do once.
#
# # What it does
#
# 1. **The HVF leg on `main`'s tip, first and separately** (`script/ci-build hvf`). Reported and never
#    blocking: it is the one check GitHub's runners cannot run (notes/hvf-leg.md), so this is a
#    convenient place for it, but a red there is about `main`, not about any queue entry.
# 2. **A baseline on `main`'s tip**, the same ladder as below with the aarch64 suite forced on. If
#    `main` is red, every entry would inherit its failure and none could be blamed for its own, so
#    the script **acts on nothing and exits 3**.
# 3. **The walk.** `repository.mergeQueue(branch: "main")` in queue order, then open pull requests
#    with auto-merge armed that are not yet queued. In one reusable detached scratch worktree
#    (`~/projects/nife-worktrees/preflight`, or `$PREFLIGHT_WORKTREE`), each entry is merged onto
#    the green entries ahead of it. A conflict is a finding. Otherwise the ladder runs, cheapest
#    first, and stops at the first red:
#
#        script/ci-build fmt
#        script/ci-build lint
#        cargo test -p documentation
#        cargo test -p <each host crate the entry touches>
#        script/test --arch aarch64                          only if the entry touches code
#        VERIFY_JOBS=2 script/falsifications --affected-since <stack>   only if no cbmc is running
#                                                    and `script/verify --affected-since` says the
#                                                    proofs are in scope, which is CI's own condition
#
#    "Touches code" is CI's own documentation-only predicate, read out of `.github/workflows/ci.yml`
#    at run time rather than copied, and the script refuses to run if the copies there disagree.
#    A red entry is dropped from the stack, so the entries behind it are tested the way the queue
#    will test them once it has been removed.
# 4. **With `--act`**, each red entry gets a comment (the failing command, an excerpt, and the stack
#    it was tested on) and is dequeued, or has auto-merge disabled if it was armed but not queued.
#    Without `--act` nothing on GitHub changes.
# 5. **A table at the end**, one row per entry. Exit 0 all green, 1 at least one finding, 2 a
#    refusal (usage, a held disk image, disagreeing predicates), 3 `main` is red.
#
# Logs go under `$TMPDIR/preflight-queue/<run>/`, never into a checkout: a transcript written into
# the tree it is testing is how the weekly falsification sweep swept nothing for three weeks
# (notes/corrections/2026-09-23-the-sweep-that-swept-nothing.md, once that record lands).
#
# # What it skips, and why
#
# - **`script/verify`.** About 47 minutes of Kani and 3.5 GB per solver; AGENTS.md allows one at a
#   time on this machine. The affected-since falsification rung is the part of the proof machinery
#   that costs seconds, and it is skipped too when `pgrep -f cbmc` finds a solver already running.
# - **The riscv64 and x86_64 legs.** One architecture is enough to catch a stack that does not build
#   or boot, and the other two roughly triple the walk. The queue's group build still runs them.
# - **`cpu-matrix`, `bench`, `fuzz`, `coverage`.** `ci`-tier rows in `script/ci-build`'s table, each
#   priced there as too slow for a person to wait for; this walk is that person, once per entry.
#
# # BUGS
#
# - **It approximates the cumulative merge; it does not reproduce it.** GitHub builds a merge group
#   from its own merge of each entry onto the one ahead, and this builds `git merge --no-ff` of each
#   head onto a local stack. The two agree on content whenever neither conflicts, which is the case
#   that matters, but a group's commit is not this script's commit, and a red that depends on the
#   exact merge base could differ.
# - **It cannot see reorders after it runs.** The queue changes while this walks it (an entry lands,
#   one is dequeued, a new one is armed), and a result is only true of the order it was taken in.
#   The table prints that order, so a reader can tell whether it is still the queue.
# - **A dry run keeps its reds in the queue but not in its own stack.** Entries behind a red one are
#   tested as though it had been removed, which is what `--act` does and what a failed group does.
#   Until someone acts, the real queue will build them with it.
# - **It takes the `nife-dev` toolchain link**, because `script/test` relinks it to whichever worktree
#   ran `xtask std-src` last (notes/std.md). On exit it puts the link back where it found it, or on
#   the main checkout's farm if that lane's worktree was pruned during the walk, and only if the link
#   still points into the scratch worktree: a lane that took it in the meantime keeps it.
#   A lane gating at the same moment can still be crossed, which is the ambient hazard AGENTS.md
#   names, not a new one.
# - **A red is attributed to the entry, not to the pair.** "Fails on top of the entries ahead" can
#   mean the entry is broken alone or that it conflicts semantically with one ahead of it; this does
#   not re-test it alone on `main` to say which. Its own pull request's CI already answers that.
# - **The falsification rung is not a required check in CI** (verify.yml's `falsify` job says so),
#   so a red there would not fail a merge group. It is on the ladder because a survivor is a real
#   defect in the proofs, and `--act` treats it like any other red; a reader who disagrees should
#   know that is the one rung stricter than the queue.
# - **One falsification can take half an hour.** `nifefs::a_short_image_is_refused_not_indexed` and
#   `package_archive::a_short_file_is_refused` run 20 to 31 minutes each at over 3 GB (verify.yml
#   records it), so an entry that reaches either crate makes this walk that much longer.
# - **Host tests are per touched crate, not per dependent.** A change to a crate's API that breaks a
#   dependent is caught by the aarch64 suite rung (whose host pass is the whole workspace) or by
#   lint's clippy pass, not by this rung.
#
# # Refusals
#
# It refuses if another run of it holds the scratch worktree, or if any process has its working
# directory inside it (a person running a gate there by hand; its first run met exactly that). And
# it refuses if any process holds the scratch worktree's `target/nifefs.img` (`lsof`), because a
# QEMU holding it fails the next suite with a lock error that looks like a bug in the entry under
# test (AGENTS.md, "Never leave QEMU running"). Every rung runs in its own process group with a time
# bound, and on exit every QEMU whose command line names the scratch worktree is killed at the root.
#
# Name: provisional, minted 2026-09-24 for this lane. `preflight` is the aviation word for the
# checks run before a thing leaves the ground, and a merge group leaving is the moment it guards;
# `queue` says which thing. Hyphenated, as shell entry points here are (script/ci-build's block).
# calef has not ruled on it.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
. helpers/qemu-path.sh

exec python3 - "$@" <<'PY'
import atexit
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time

ROOT = os.getcwd()
ACT = False
HVF = True
for a in sys.argv[1:]:
    if a == "--act":
        ACT = True
    elif a == "--dry-run":
        ACT = False
    elif a == "--no-hvf":
        HVF = False
    elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
        print("usage: script/preflight-queue [--dry-run | --act] [--no-hvf]")
        sys.exit(0)
    else:
        print("script/preflight-queue: unknown option %r" % a, file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)

WT = os.path.expanduser(os.environ.get("PREFLIGHT_WORKTREE", "~/projects/nife-worktrees/preflight"))
RUN = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime())
LOGS = os.path.join(os.environ.get("TMPDIR", "/tmp"), "preflight-queue", RUN)
os.makedirs(LOGS, exist_ok=True)
# Whose account GitHub shows is whoever `gh` is logged in as, read rather than spelled, because more
# than one architect can run this (ARCHITECTS.md).
ACCOUNT = subprocess.run(["gh", "api", "user", "--jq", ".login"], capture_output=True,
                         text=True).stdout.strip() or "the `gh` user"
LANE = f"**Lane:** script/preflight-queue; {ACCOUNT}'s account is the author GitHub shows."

# Bounds, in seconds, per rung. Generous against the measured costs so that only a hang trips them.
BOUND = {"fmt": 300, "lint": 1800, "documentation": 900, "host": 1800,
         "aarch64": 2400, "falsifications": 3600, "hvf": 2400}


def say(msg):
    print("preflight-queue: " + msg, flush=True)


def sh(args, cwd=ROOT, check=True):
    r = subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    if check and r.returncode != 0:
        say("command failed: %s\n%s" % (" ".join(args), r.stderr.strip()))
        sys.exit(2)
    return r


def git(*args, check=True):
    return sh(["git", "-C", WT] + list(args), check=check)


# --- leaving the machine as it was found --------------------------------------------------------

def nife_dev_link():
    r = sh(["rustup", "toolchain", "list", "-v"], check=False)
    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("nife-dev"):
            return line.split(None, 1)[1].strip() if len(line.split(None, 1)) > 1 else None
    return None


LINK_BEFORE = nife_dev_link()
# The main checkout, which owns the canonical farm: the parent of the shared `.git`.
ROOT_CHECKOUT = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(subprocess.run(
    ["git", "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"], capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=ROOT).stdout.strip()))


def kill_our_qemus():
    """Kill every QEMU whose command line names the scratch worktree. Returns the survivors."""
    r = sh(["pgrep", "-f", "qemu-system.*" + re.escape(WT)], check=False)
    pids = [int(p) for p in r.stdout.split()]
    for p in pids:
        try:
            os.kill(p, signal.SIGTERM)
        except ProcessLookupError:
            pass
    if pids:
        time.sleep(2)
    return sh(["pgrep", "-f", "qemu-system.*" + re.escape(WT)], check=False).stdout.split()


def cleanup():
    left = kill_our_qemus()
    if left:
        say("WARNING: QEMU still running after SIGTERM: %s" % " ".join(left))
    now = nife_dev_link()
    if not (now and now.startswith(WT)):
        if now != LINK_BEFORE:
            say("nife-dev now points at %s, not taken by this run; left alone" % now)
        return
    # **Put it back somewhere that exists, and say so only once rustup agrees.** Its first proof
    # run (2026-09-24) "relinked" to the farm of a lane worktree that had been pruned while the walk
    # ran; rustup refused, the exit code was not read, and the message claimed success while the link
    # still pointed into the scratch tree. So: the link as found if that farm still exists, else the
    # main checkout's farm, which is where briefs/merge-and-cleanup.md relinks it anyway.
    main_farm = os.path.join(ROOT_CHECKOUT, "target", "nife-farm")
    target = next((t for t in (LINK_BEFORE, main_farm) if t and os.path.isdir(t)), None)
    if target is None:
        say("nife-dev still points into %s: neither %s nor %s exists. Relink it from the main "
            "checkout (briefs/merge-and-cleanup.md step 4)" % (WT, LINK_BEFORE, main_farm))
        return
    r = subprocess.run(["rustup", "toolchain", "link", "nife-dev", target],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    if r.returncode == 0 and nife_dev_link() == target:
        say("nife-dev relinked to %s (the scratch worktree had taken it%s)" %
            (target, "" if target == LINK_BEFORE else "; the farm it pointed at before is gone"))
    else:
        say("nife-dev relink to %s FAILED (%s); it still points at %s" %
            (target, (r.stderr or "rustup exited %d" % r.returncode).strip(), nife_dev_link()))

atexit.register(cleanup)
signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda *_: sys.exit(130))
signal.signal(signal.SIGHUP, lambda *_: sys.exit(130))

# --- CI's documentation-only predicate, read rather than copied ---------------------------------

ci = open(os.path.join(ROOT, ".github/workflows/ci.yml")).read()
preds = set(re.findall(r"grep -vE '([^']+)'", ci))
if len(preds) != 1:
    say("refusing: .github/workflows/ci.yml carries %d distinct documentation-only predicates "
        "(%s); expected exactly one, shared by every job" % (len(preds), ", ".join(sorted(preds)) or "none"))
    sys.exit(2)
DOC = re.compile(preds.pop())


def docs_only(files):
    return all(DOC.match(f) for f in files)


# --- the scratch worktree --------------------------------------------------------------------------

common = os.path.realpath(sh(["git", "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"]).stdout.strip())
sh(["git", "fetch", "-q", "origin", "main"])
MAIN = sh(["git", "rev-parse", "origin/main"]).stdout.strip()

# **One run per scratch worktree, and nobody else inside it.** Found by this script's own first run
# (2026-09-24): a person was running `script/test --hvf` by hand in the same scratch worktree, and this
# script's `reset --hard` and the two runs' rebuilds of one `nife-dev` farm crossed, failing an HVF
# leg with missing std sources that looked like a red `main`. So two checks, both before any reset:
# an exclusive lock that a second run of this script cannot take, and a refusal if any process at all
# has its working directory inside the scratch tree, which is what catches a person who is not this
# script. The second is the one that would have caught the first collision.
LOCK = open(os.path.join(common, "preflight-queue-%s.lock" % os.path.basename(WT)), "w")
try:
    fcntl.flock(LOCK, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
except OSError:
    say("refusing: another script/preflight-queue run holds %s" % WT)
    sys.exit(2)
cwds = sh(["lsof", "-d", "cwd", "-Fpn"], check=False).stdout.splitlines()
inside, pid = [], None
for line in cwds:
    if line.startswith("p"):
        pid = line[1:]
    elif line.startswith("n") and pid != str(os.getpid()) and \
            (line[1:] == WT or line[1:].startswith(WT.rstrip("/") + "/")):
        inside.append(pid)
if inside:
    say("refusing: process(es) %s have their working directory inside %s, so somebody is using it. "
        "Set PREFLIGHT_WORKTREE to run elsewhere, or wait" % (" ".join(sorted(set(inside))), WT))
    sys.exit(2)

if os.path.isdir(WT):
    theirs = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(WT, sh(["git", "-C", WT, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"]).stdout.strip()))
    if theirs != common:
        say("refusing: %s is not a worktree of this repository" % WT)
        sys.exit(2)
    if git("symbolic-ref", "-q", "HEAD", check=False).returncode == 0:
        # A branch checked out here means a person is using it; the scratch tree is always detached.
        say("refusing: %s has a branch checked out, so it is somebody's, not this script's" % WT)
        sys.exit(2)
else:
    sh(["git", "worktree", "add", "-q", "--detach", WT, MAIN])


def reset_to(sha):
    # `reset --hard` is safe here and nowhere else: this worktree is detached, owned by this script,
    # and holds nothing but merges it made itself. `clean -fd` without `-x` keeps target/ warm.
    git("merge", "--abort", check=False)
    git("reset", "-q", "--hard", sha)
    git("clean", "-q", "-fd")


reset_to(MAIN)


def image_holders():
    img = os.path.join(WT, "target", "nifefs.img")
    if not os.path.exists(img):
        return []
    return sh(["lsof", "-t", img], check=False).stdout.split()


held = image_holders()
if held:
    say("refusing: %s/target/nifefs.img is held by pid %s (lsof). A QEMU holding it fails the next "
        "suite with a lock error; find its parent with `ps -o pid,ppid,command` and kill the tree at "
        "its root" % (WT, " ".join(held)))
    sys.exit(2)

# --- host crates, derived the way script/lint's host-pass gate derives them ----------------------

meta = json.loads(sh(["cargo", "metadata", "--format-version", "1"], cwd=WT).stdout)
ws_root = meta["workspace_root"]
ws_ids = set(meta["workspace_members"])
by_id = {p["id"]: p for p in meta["packages"]}
deps = {n["id"]: {d["pkg"] for d in n.get("deps", [])} for n in meta["resolve"]["nodes"]}
# `user_mode_runtime` and everything that reaches it cannot compile for the host (EL0 syscall `asm!`),
# plus the three packages that are not host code at all. The same set `xtask test` excludes.
bare = {pid for pid in ws_ids if by_id[pid]["name"] == "user_mode_runtime"}
grew = True
while grew:
    grew = False
    for pid in ws_ids - bare:
        if deps.get(pid, set()) & bare:
            bare.add(pid)
            grew = True
bare |= {pid for pid in ws_ids if by_id[pid]["name"] in ("kernel", "components", "fixtures")}
HOST = {}
for pid in ws_ids - bare:
    rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.dirname(by_id[pid]["manifest_path"]), ws_root)
    if rel != ".":
        HOST[rel + "/"] = by_id[pid]["name"]


def touched_host_crates(files):
    out = set()
    for f in files:
        best = max((p for p in HOST if f.startswith(p)), key=len, default=None)
        if best:
            out.add(HOST[best])
    return sorted(out)


# --- running a rung --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def run(label, tag, cmd, bound, env=None):
    """Run one rung in its own process group, bounded. Returns (ok, seconds, log path)."""
    log = os.path.join(LOGS, "%s-%s.log" % (label, tag))
    t = time.time()
    with open(log, "w") as fh:
        fh.write("$ %s\n" % cmd)
        fh.flush()
        p = subprocess.Popen(["sh", "-c", cmd], cwd=WT, stdout=fh, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                             stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True,
                             env=dict(os.environ, **(env or {})))
        try:
            rc = p.wait(timeout=bound)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            # Kill the tree at its root: the process group reaches cargo, xtask and the QEMU under
            # them, where signalling `sh` alone would orphan them (AGENTS.md, 2026-08-02).
            os.killpg(p.pid, signal.SIGTERM)
            try:
                p.wait(timeout=20)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                os.killpg(p.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
                p.wait()
            fh.write("\npreflight-queue: BOUND of %ds exceeded; process group killed\n" % bound)
            rc = 124
    kill_our_qemus()
    return rc == 0, round(time.time() - t), log


def excerpt(log, n=30):
    lines = open(log, errors="replace").read().splitlines()
    hits = [l for l in lines if re.search(r"error|FAIL|panicked|failed|Diff in|BOUND", l)]
    pick = (hits[-n:] if hits else lines[-n:])
    return "\n".join(pick)[-5000:]


def ladder(label, files, base, force_code=False):
    """The rungs, cheapest first; stop at the first red. Returns (result, rung, cmd, log, seconds)."""
    code = force_code or not docs_only(files)
    rungs = [("fmt", "script/ci-build fmt", BOUND["fmt"], None),
             ("lint", "script/ci-build lint", BOUND["lint"], None),
             ("documentation", "cargo test -p documentation", BOUND["documentation"], None)]
    crates = touched_host_crates(files) if not force_code else []
    if crates:
        rungs.append(("host", "cargo test " + " ".join("-p " + c for c in crates), BOUND["host"], None))
    if code:
        rungs.append(("aarch64", "script/test --arch aarch64", BOUND["aarch64"], None))
    if code and not force_code:
        rungs.append(("falsifications", "script/falsifications --affected-since " + base,
                      BOUND["falsifications"], {"VERIFY_JOBS": "2"}))
    total = 0
    notes = []
    for tag, cmd, bound, env in rungs:
        if tag == "aarch64" and image_holders():
            return ("refused", tag, cmd, None, total, "target/nifefs.img is held (lsof)")
        if tag == "falsifications":
            if sh(["pgrep", "-f", "cbmc"], check=False).stdout.strip():
                notes.append("falsifications skipped: cbmc running")
                continue
            # CI runs this rung only when `script/verify --affected-since` says the proofs are in
            # scope (verify.yml's `scope` job), and so does this. Without it, a change to a path no
            # package owns (`briefs/`, say) reaches every harness package, and the first dry run
            # spent over twenty minutes re-falsifying all 74 records for a documentation edit.
            scope = sh(["script/verify", "--affected-since", base], cwd=WT, check=False).stdout.strip()
            if not scope.endswith("verify-scope: needed"):
                notes.append("falsifications out of verify's scope")
                continue
        say("  %s: %s" % (label, cmd))
        ok, secs, log = run(label, tag, cmd, bound, env)
        total += secs
        if not ok:
            return ("red", tag, cmd, log, total, "; ".join(notes))
    return ("green", "all" if code else "docs", "", None, total, "; ".join(notes))


# --- the queue ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

repo = sh(["gh", "repo", "view", "--json", "nameWithOwner", "-q", ".nameWithOwner"]).stdout.strip()
owner, name = repo.split("/")
Q = """query($o:String!,$n:String!){repository(owner:$o,name:$n){
  mergeQueue(branch:"main"){entries(first:100){nodes{position pullRequest{id number title headRefName headRefOid}}}}
  pullRequests(states:OPEN,first:100){nodes{id number title isDraft headRefName headRefOid
    autoMergeRequest{enabledAt} mergeQueueEntry{position}}}}}"""
data = json.loads(sh(["gh", "api", "graphql", "-f", "query=" + Q, "-f", "o=" + owner, "-f", "n=" + name]).stdout)
r = data["data"]["repository"]
entries = []
for e in sorted(r["mergeQueue"]["entries"]["nodes"], key=lambda e: e["position"]):
    entries.append(dict(e["pullRequest"], kind="queued #%d" % e["position"]))
armed = [p for p in r["pullRequests"]["nodes"]
         if p["autoMergeRequest"] and not p["mergeQueueEntry"] and not p["isDraft"]]
for p in sorted(armed, key=lambda p: p["autoMergeRequest"]["enabledAt"]):
    entries.append(dict(p, kind="armed"))
say("%d queued, %d armed-but-unqueued; main is %s; logs in %s" %
    (len(r["mergeQueue"]["entries"]["nodes"]), len(armed), MAIN[:9], LOGS))

rows = []
PRINTED = []


def print_table():
    """The table, printed once: at the end, or on the way out of an interrupted walk, so a run
    killed after an hour still says what it found."""
    if PRINTED:
        return
    PRINTED.append(True)
    print()
    print("| entry | where | head | scope | result | stopped at | seconds |")
    print("|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|")
    for row in rows:
        print("| %s | %s | `%s` | %s | %s | %s | %s |" % row)
    print(flush=True)


atexit.register(print_table)

# --- 1. the HVF leg on main's tip, reported and never blocking ------------------------------------

if HVF:
    say("HVF leg on main's tip (%s), reported only" % MAIN[:9])
    ok, secs, log = run("main", "hvf", "script/ci-build hvf", BOUND["hvf"])
    text = open(log, errors="replace").read()
    hvf = "skipped" if "SKIPPED the HVF leg" in text else ("green" if ok else "red")
    rows.append(("main", "HVF leg", MAIN[:9], "code", hvf, "hvf", secs))
    if hvf == "red":
        say("HVF leg is red on main; not blocking. Excerpt:\n" + excerpt(log, 15))
    reset_to(MAIN)

# --- 2. the baseline: is main itself green? -------------------------------------------------------

say("baseline on main's tip")
res, rung, cmd, log, secs, note = ladder("main", [], MAIN, force_code=True)
rows.append(("main", "baseline", MAIN[:9], "code", res, rung, secs))
if res != "green":
    say("main is %s at %s (`%s`); acting on nothing.\n%s" % (res, rung, cmd, excerpt(log) if log else note))
    sys.exit(3)

# --- 3. the walk -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

stack = MAIN
ahead = []
findings = []
ident = ["-c", "user.name=preflight-queue", "-c", "user.email=preflight-queue@localhost"]
for e in entries:
    n = e["number"]
    label = "pr%d" % n
    fetched = git("fetch", "-q", "origin", e["headRefOid"], check=False)
    if fetched.returncode != 0:
        rows.append(("#%d" % n, e["kind"], e["headRefOid"][:9], "?", "error", "fetch", 0))
        continue
    m = sh(["git", "-C", WT] + ident + ["merge", "-q", "--no-ff", "--no-edit", "-m",
            "preflight: #%d %s" % (n, e["headRefName"]), e["headRefOid"]], check=False)
    if m.returncode != 0:
        conflicted = git("diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=U", check=False).stdout.split()
        reset_to(stack)
        findings.append(dict(e, rung="merge", cmd="git merge %s onto the stack" % e["headRefOid"][:9],
                             text="conflicts in: " + (", ".join(conflicted) or m.stdout.strip()[-500:]),
                             stack=list(ahead)))
        rows.append(("#%d" % n, e["kind"], e["headRefOid"][:9], "?", "CONFLICT", "merge", 0))
        continue
    files = git("diff", "--name-only", stack, "HEAD").stdout.split()
    scope = "docs" if docs_only(files) else "code"
    res, rung, cmd, log, secs, note = ladder(label, files, stack)
    rows.append(("#%d" % n, e["kind"], e["headRefOid"][:9], scope, res.upper() if res != "green" else res,
                 rung + (" (%s)" % note if note else ""), secs))
    if res == "green":
        stack = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
        ahead.append((n, e["headRefOid"]))
    else:
        if res == "red":
            findings.append(dict(e, rung=rung, cmd=cmd, text=excerpt(log), stack=list(ahead)))
        reset_to(stack)

# --- 4. acting on what is red for its own reason --------------------------------------------------

for f in findings:
    stack_text = "`main` at `%s`" % MAIN[:9] + "".join(", then #%d (`%s`)" % (n, s[:9]) for n, s in f["stack"])
    body = "\n".join([
        LANE, "",
        "**`script/preflight-queue` found this red on top of the entries ahead of it**, which per-PR CI "
        "cannot see and a merge group of five would have found expensively.", "",
        "- **Failing command:** `%s`" % f["cmd"],
        "- **Stack tested:** %s, then this pull request at `%s`." % (stack_text, f["headRefOid"][:9]),
        "", "```", f["text"], "```", "",
        ("It has been %s. Re-arm it once it is green on top of the current queue."
         % ("dequeued" if f["kind"] != "armed" else "disarmed (auto-merge disabled)")),
    ])
    if not ACT:
        continue
    subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "comment", str(f["number"]), "--repo", repo, "--body", body],
                   capture_output=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    if f["kind"] == "armed":
        subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "merge", str(f["number"]), "--repo", repo, "--disable-auto"],
                       capture_output=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    else:
        subprocess.run(["gh", "api", "graphql", "-f",
                        "query=mutation($id:ID!){dequeuePullRequest(input:{id:$id}){clientMutationId}}",
                        "-f", "id=" + f["id"]], capture_output=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)

# --- 5. the table ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

print_table()
for f in findings:
    print("#%d red at %s: %s" % (f["number"], f["rung"], f["cmd"]))
say("%d finding(s); %s; logs in %s" % (len(findings), "acted on" if ACT else "dry run, nothing changed on GitHub", LOGS))
sys.exit(1 if findings else 0)
PY
